TOTK: the AST optimizer leaves warp-collective operations (ballot, vote, shuffle) where the shader put them. It propagated
an expression into each of its uses, and a ballot moved into a divergent branch saw only that branch's lanes. The NVN2
compiler's warp-aggregated atomics take one VOTEU of the lanes that append, pick the leader from it (FLO) and, in the
leader's branch, add the vote's POPC to the counter: with the ballot folded into the POPC the leader counted only itself,
each warp reserved one slot and its other lanes wrote over the next warps' entries. OoT's GPU culling
(FoliageShader) kept a fraction of its lists: tree crowns, bushes and most shadow casters were missing, and the fog
lit through the empty shadow cascades washed the frame out. Maxwell jobs get the same rule (a moved vote is wrong there
too).

